1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,400
Aarav plays his favourite song
through a small amplifier.

2
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:05,736
The singer sounds loud and clear.

3
00:00:06,036 --> 00:00:08,280
But the deep drum beats sound weak.

4
00:00:08,280 --> 00:00:10,042
The high whistles fade too.

5
00:00:10,342 --> 00:00:13,962
Why does one amplifier boost
some sounds better than others?

6
00:00:14,462 --> 00:00:17,386
Think of a pipe with a rubber
wall stretched across it.

7
00:00:17,736 --> 00:00:19,770
Push the water back and forth fast.

8
00:00:19,770 --> 00:00:22,318
The wall wiggles, and
the wave gets through.

9
00:00:22,668 --> 00:00:26,583
Push very slowly, and the wall
just stretches and stops the flow.

10
00:00:26,933 --> 00:00:29,459
A capacitor acts like this rubber wall.

11
00:00:29,809 --> 00:00:32,900
It blocks steady current,
but lets a quick wave pass.

12
00:00:33,550 --> 00:00:36,042
Now picture tiny holes in the pipe wall.

13
00:00:36,392 --> 00:00:39,597
At very fast wiggles, water
leaks out through them.

14
00:00:39,597 --> 00:00:41,032
Less reaches the end.

15
00:00:41,682 --> 00:00:44,759
Let's build a CE amplifier
on the breadboard.

16
00:00:45,109 --> 00:00:49,112
It starts with the voltage divider
bias from the last episode.

17
00:00:49,462 --> 00:00:52,790
The signal comes in
through a capacitor, C in.

18
00:00:53,140 --> 00:00:56,722
It blocks the steady voltage,
so the bias stays safe.

19
00:00:57,072 --> 00:01:00,749
C out passes the big
wave on to the load, RL.

20
00:01:01,099 --> 00:01:03,121
And CE sits across RE.

21
00:01:03,121 --> 00:01:05,176
It is the bypass capacitor.

22
00:01:05,526 --> 00:01:09,346
The wave skips past RE through
it, so the gain stays high.

23
00:01:09,996 --> 00:01:13,554
Now feed in a ten millivolt
wave at one kilohertz.

24
00:01:13,904 --> 00:01:16,291
The output is one point five volts.

25
00:01:16,291 --> 00:01:18,333
That is a gain of one fifty.

26
00:01:18,683 --> 00:01:20,114
And it is upside down.

27
00:01:20,114 --> 00:01:22,029
One eighty degrees out of phase.

28
00:01:22,679 --> 00:01:26,363
Now turn the frequency knob
down, to a deep bass note.

29
00:01:26,713 --> 00:01:30,050
At twenty hertz, the output
shrinks to about half a volt.

30
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:32,632
Now turn it up, to a very high whistle.

31
00:01:32,982 --> 00:01:35,715
At one megahertz, the
output shrinks again.

32
00:01:36,365 --> 00:01:38,748
Plot the gain at every frequency.

33
00:01:38,748 --> 00:01:40,963
This is the frequency response.

34
00:01:41,313 --> 00:01:43,427
In the middle, the gain is flat.

35
00:01:43,427 --> 00:01:44,855
This is the mid band.

36
00:01:45,205 --> 00:01:46,685
At low frequencies, the
capacitors fight back.

37
00:01:47,035 --> 00:01:51,280
Their opposition, called reactance,
grows as the frequency falls.

38
00:01:51,630 --> 00:01:55,175
So C in, C out and CE stop
passing the wave well.

39
00:01:55,825 --> 00:01:59,012
At high frequencies, tiny hidden
capacitors inside the transistor take

40
00:01:59,012 --> 00:01:59,912
over.

41
00:01:59,782 --> 00:02:02,130
Even the wires act like small capacitors.

42
00:02:02,130 --> 00:02:03,951
The wave leaks away to ground.

43
00:02:04,601 --> 00:02:08,882
Mark where the gain drops to zero
point seven zero seven of the middle

44
00:02:08,882 --> 00:02:09,782
value.

45
00:02:09,827 --> 00:02:11,580
The lower one is f one.

46
00:02:11,580 --> 00:02:13,332
The upper one is f two.

47
00:02:13,682 --> 00:02:16,589
The band between them is the bandwidth.

48
00:02:16,589 --> 00:02:19,008
BW equals f two minus f one.

49
00:02:19,658 --> 00:02:21,085
Big in the middle.

50
00:02:21,085 --> 00:02:22,512
Weak at both ends.

51
00:02:23,312 --> 00:02:25,563
Now the maths, one step at a time.

52
00:02:25,913 --> 00:02:31,248
Inside the transistor, the emitter
acts like a tiny resistance, r e prime.

53
00:02:31,598 --> 00:02:35,371
r e prime equals twenty six
millivolts, divided by IE.

54
00:02:36,071 --> 00:02:39,991
At the output, RC and RL
share the wave side by side.

55
00:02:40,341 --> 00:02:44,931
So r c equals RC times
RL, over RC plus RL.

56
00:02:45,631 --> 00:02:50,114
The voltage gain AV equals
r c, divided by r e prime.

57
00:02:50,814 --> 00:02:54,391
The minus sign in front means
the output is upside down.

58
00:02:55,091 --> 00:02:58,251
In decibels, the gain equals
twenty times the log of AV.

59
00:02:58,951 --> 00:03:03,394
At a cut off, the gain is zero point
seven zero seven times its middle

60
00:03:03,394 --> 00:03:04,294
value.

61
00:03:04,340 --> 00:03:06,156
That is three decibels down.

62
00:03:06,156 --> 00:03:08,260
The output power is exactly half.

63
00:03:08,910 --> 00:03:12,295
So f one and f two are also
called the half power frequencies.

64
00:03:12,945 --> 00:03:13,952
Worked example.

65
00:03:13,952 --> 00:03:15,940
Find the gain and the bandwidth.

66
00:03:16,290 --> 00:03:18,293
The bias sets IE at two milliamperes.

67
00:03:18,643 --> 00:03:22,339
RC and RL are both three
point nine kilo ohms.

68
00:03:22,689 --> 00:03:25,181
r e prime is twenty six over two.

69
00:03:25,181 --> 00:03:26,845
That is thirteen ohms.

70
00:03:27,545 --> 00:03:31,464
r c is the two three point
nine kilo ohms in parallel.

71
00:03:31,814 --> 00:03:34,404
That is one point nine five kilo ohms.

72
00:03:35,104 --> 00:03:38,234
AV is one thousand nine
fifty over thirteen.

73
00:03:38,234 --> 00:03:40,138
That is one hundred fifty.

74
00:03:40,838 --> 00:03:44,435
In decibels, twenty log one fifty
is about forty three point five.

75
00:03:45,135 --> 00:03:48,488
The cut offs are fifty hertz
and two hundred kilohertz.

76
00:03:48,838 --> 00:03:51,821
So the bandwidth is about
two hundred kilohertz.

77
00:03:52,471 --> 00:03:53,933
Now, the exam trap.

78
00:03:54,283 --> 00:03:57,207
Many students say the gain
falls to half at the cut off.

79
00:03:57,557 --> 00:03:58,591
It does not.

80
00:03:58,591 --> 00:04:02,516
The voltage gain falls to zero
point seven zero seven times.

81
00:04:03,166 --> 00:04:05,134
It is the power that falls to half.

82
00:04:05,134 --> 00:04:07,222
That is why it is three decibels down.

83
00:04:07,872 --> 00:04:09,496
Where is this asked?

84
00:04:09,496 --> 00:04:11,360
In PUC board papers.

85
00:04:12,010 --> 00:04:16,883
Draw the CE amplifier and its
frequency response, and find the gain.

86
00:04:17,533 --> 00:04:18,433
Try this.

87
00:04:18,356 --> 00:04:21,767
An amplifier has a mid band
gain of forty decibels.

88
00:04:21,767 --> 00:04:25,078
f one is forty hertz, f
two is forty kilohertz.

89
00:04:25,078 --> 00:04:28,078
Find the gain at f two, and the bandwidth.

90
00:04:33,478 --> 00:04:34,378
B.

91
00:04:33,879 --> 00:04:37,844
Seventy point seven, and thirty
nine point nine six kilohertz.

92
00:04:38,194 --> 00:04:41,112
Forty decibels means
twenty log A equals forty.

93
00:04:41,112 --> 00:04:42,580
So A is one hundred.

94
00:04:42,930 --> 00:04:47,659
At f two, the gain is zero point
seven zero seven times one hundred.

95
00:04:47,659 --> 00:04:49,132
Seventy point seven.

96
00:04:49,482 --> 00:04:52,960
The bandwidth is forty
kilohertz minus forty hertz.

97
00:04:52,960 --> 00:04:55,568
Thirty nine point nine six kilohertz.

98
00:04:56,218 --> 00:04:57,765
Screenshot this card.

99
00:05:00,965 --> 00:05:04,016
Next, the RC coupled multistage amplifier.

100
00:05:04,016 --> 00:05:06,555
How do we get a gain of thousands?
